#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091871 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "47cd3aef-8196-5e95-93f9-14c5f2ec7a40" :question/math-academy-id "e-10034" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/c86e5a3dd066efc462635310ff80973f468d04bad9206f410a74941ccf39f2f3.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2$  (shown above) at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r = 2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "2a08ba95-78ee-5a32-b785-48f3a2dd037b" :question/math-academy-id "e-628" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/19b420204caf7f2706b4d355b5538dc347b5377d2b9f99297dbf6accc3220f59.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = \\theta,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\nx = \\theta\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=\\theta\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092104 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "daec9915-9b7d-5029-9461-385ca8125d3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9948" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/a0eb20697f72b1ec5f06cc1ebfa4ba364c1023c915950b64cfab2367964db928.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta) .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092105 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/id #uuid "1c8315d1-78ec-5f4f-90f3-1f7cffff712a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9949" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta  - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83df43ff-9435-5357-bd1d-7ff199a32de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337873} {:answer/id #uuid "f98d44f0-7b4a-5635-b495-46c448d6942d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337874} {:answer/id #uuid "d53fa68f-be91-5d2f-9618-397a903e7c69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337875} {:answer/id #uuid "ccff7b2c-3b07-5ce2-a21b-452d728476f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337876} {:answer/id #uuid "62c9bcc6-83f2-5cb0-8c83-7520c5749b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62c9bcc6-83f2-5cb0-8c83-7520c5749b8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337877} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a89ace6-8c09-5d39-8891-a6a0a0eb2612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ead23169-92f1-547c-8ee1-48f1193b6177" :question/math-academy-id "q-141036" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/ab95faf73fa837d232a67a773afdbf456f5798d0f05de23e6ce44b41cddb1881.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52bdf10-cae3-5b8f-9b97-cc65bb6071a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337880} {:answer/id #uuid "7355c7ca-7cf2-5bba-9730-754374dd99ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337881} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9ffe91-ed4f-55c6-90a8-e22522060083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337882} {:answer/id #uuid "1356b4f0-7145-5e80-9e86-97439e8c7e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337883} {:answer/id #uuid "896c026b-59b6-57bb-a1cc-de6326bd98db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52bdf10-cae3-5b8f-9b97-cc65bb6071a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337880} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5633fec-b5a9-5f52-b351-7c9c31580e97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2c690f6-8c89-5be9-abe4-ae0d85823733" :question/math-academy-id "q-141040" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/dcb8b5dee06f59322f2f365512065857c79715be39f96a50d91cc9062991aa6e.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=10{e}^{-\\theta }$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{-10{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f2f47f4-aa3c-590c-81b2-92caf242497a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337887} {:answer/id #uuid "2fda00af-63ce-5268-b73c-092e60cb1f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337888} {:answer/id #uuid "c97dfd70-8f98-52fc-8df9-bdf4ad4e190a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337889} {:answer/id #uuid "aba4ef22-40bb-5bcc-aca4-1eaa383c4290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337890} {:answer/id #uuid "62cac228-a0a4-5c20-a17d-3470c4b9fb12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f2f47f4-aa3c-590c-81b2-92caf242497a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337887} :answer-field/id #uuid "878319dc-5f9b-5a6b-a67e-192061f68d82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32f5cae8-0afe-508b-ace8-3e3628e8d1ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-141046" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{e}^{\\pi /2})} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2ccbb8c-c20d-541e-bfd7-5f2cb60cd442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337894} {:answer/id #uuid "2cadc5d0-c630-5742-aebc-b17b7ba68094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337895} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb0f58b-ca94-5b3a-8b45-13641a121ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337896} {:answer/id #uuid "2dbfd437-651e-5e1b-bd59-6975203c0e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337897} {:answer/id #uuid "526491f5-17fc-5966-8c7c-7a80bb92f5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dbfd437-651e-5e1b-bd59-6975203c0e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337897} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae77d6aa-2d15-52fc-b259-9b17badb1565" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46df02d0-4baa-5092-ab18-1b78992d66ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-141048" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=2\\theta $  at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =2+0 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337899 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a06522a-c63d-593c-b997-6387b80f303a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337901} {:answer/id #uuid "27d8a01d-fe66-59c5-a298-9a6e482e6f98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3\\theta }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337902} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f330fe-2ce0-5db4-bda5-a064f9255a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337903} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb17bb8-f961-5716-a380-0eb75b68ff41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337904} {:answer/id #uuid "a321c264-fb59-5bbc-ac16-4a6a43a775f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a321c264-fb59-5bbc-ac16-4a6a43a775f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337905} :answer-field/id #uuid "9318495e-67a0-566c-a041-c71cd2486668" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "deae29cc-71dd-5ba2-8057-aa58580459cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-141050" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/ed90c6464c78010f38c764a677bddd3c7da22920fe4db62769e02d05934c9d24.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r={e}^{-3\\theta },$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r={e}^{-3\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y={e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3\\theta }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={({e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={({e}^{-3\\theta })}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{-3\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\theta }(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}{-{e}^{-3\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9fd7553-30d2-5940-ac73-c78aa49154f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337908} {:answer/id #uuid "410a1425-86fd-5750-b212-210db7f027ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337909} {:answer/id #uuid "1026e018-2f6c-586e-928e-e7b9871c2f9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337910} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0a0789-3785-53cd-addf-aafd5662c01c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337911} {:answer/id #uuid "de127e39-67bc-52c6-b259-08809cc49a98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "410a1425-86fd-5750-b212-210db7f027ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337909} :answer-field/id #uuid "a78b0471-844d-5027-ad7c-a973a630a67e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6d7bb79-18ae-5765-94d3-bc45d36669b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-142369" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/40375a9794d9e399327cfcf128e017f05d20319fd86d41a8a9f49e01f58ccae0.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=4$  (shown above) at $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =7\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e1095f7-176a-553f-9294-5455d8699b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337915} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb962bf-879c-5c7f-850f-d9d5c81923bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337916} {:answer/id #uuid "39fcf485-e2a9-5066-8124-8051d46ee8ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337917} {:answer/id #uuid "572324bf-0344-556c-bf26-01b4d8419acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337918} {:answer/id #uuid "06a05e03-0901-5ea7-abb2-229bb9271d51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e1095f7-176a-553f-9294-5455d8699b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337915} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7f8ddd1-434f-5e87-bb73-ae2671805e9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c07c1e32-2396-5134-92cc-86e29114310e" :question/math-academy-id "q-142382" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=12$ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=12,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7503bb73-2829-58d8-9b5f-a5fb4c1b6f8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337922} {:answer/id #uuid "85599df8-7eeb-52cd-970a-8ebf8a4e9cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337923} {:answer/id #uuid "b994c45d-b4a7-53e9-ab5b-5347380ac0e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337924} {:answer/id #uuid "288dfff0-270c-51c7-9679-bbc03cdac03f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337925} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0b6ca5-8a54-5720-a144-52d641ce7045" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337926}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "288dfff0-270c-51c7-9679-bbc03cdac03f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337925} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c30ef90-41de-5415-9f7d-99d1094b0adb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337921}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1663e4ba-5ecf-5878-b8ad-e56044471bd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-17113" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/80c86c945ba74dca1a26c4e3c7dcd585fe57303c99d34007885d7ff4d7cfef58.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=\\theta $ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8630a673-1333-5bb2-8ece-f91be699b2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089689} {:answer/id #uuid "8f10f858-c834-5c5d-aa3e-a4c239429b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089690} {:answer/id #uuid "c91ce8bb-be48-59b1-9708-b75df0213402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ + \\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089691} {:answer/id #uuid "d21e4829-bab9-58d0-a3e7-0dbb6fc06197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos θ - \\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089692} {:answer/id #uuid "dfdb90a7-2c33-5d7f-b8eb-9ee611058381" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- (\\cos θ - \\sin θ)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f10f858-c834-5c5d-aa3e-a4c239429b57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos θ + \\sin θ}{\\cos θ - \\sin θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089690} :answer-field/id #uuid "7217078f-615c-59a4-afe1-e95bb74c82a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186089688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "303301ce-b4a4-5cfd-96c8-5b9da9450521" :question/math-academy-id "q-17115" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/dfc16cf0da1332a921899dc1d3ebcdf75a59de3d1323c83f81a61c665b135ea8.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi },$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b284742f-25d4-5bd5-8831-cd5d114f143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b284742f-25d4-5bd5-8831-cd5d114f143a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337929} :answer-field/id #uuid "ced5e725-7f07-5645-b5b2-a570c0e8eec7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f774a2d-4bc6-5fc1-ab21-b88e43fd39a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-348093" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ),$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f1eeec-927c-5411-8112-5df661e2b9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337932} {:answer/id #uuid "6576e1d1-d575-5ce0-90aa-317792fda81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cot(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f1eeec-927c-5411-8112-5df661e2b9f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337932} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6392e8d-612e-5aa3-8189-40306a768542" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79eb2daf-bacf-5407-b324-69f1a44e9c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-348096" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=18,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=18,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-18\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca21b8c9-83d4-52b7-90a4-57b4fd394350" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337936} {:answer/id #uuid "1e44ac56-dfa1-575b-8f11-fc1bda0ed005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e44ac56-dfa1-575b-8f11-fc1bda0ed005" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337937} :answer-field/id #uuid "033cf2eb-cb16-57b8-b667-9f264caff1cb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5170d88-8672-5c73-9bf3-b2239f1bac15" :question/math-academy-id "q-353218" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+{sec}^{2}⁡(\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /3} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{9}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c86c57df-5103-5931-b423-e4dafeb9721a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve at a Point Given Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c7145de-5df2-557b-ad17-e1f5e8be30e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c7145de-5df2-557b-ad17-e1f5e8be30e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337940} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c3efd92-c125-538d-b812-1c923f312509" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b959c586-f4d8-56a6-a6a1-f79e542f2117" :question/math-academy-id "q-353226" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/064e75694d82bed2177df55e2e66c3343a2c0396cd54f5d4304f952a01abb219.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\theta $ (shown above) evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$   at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76eb17b8-fa49-507f-bf5f-0bdc0d354522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76eb17b8-fa49-507f-bf5f-0bdc0d354522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337943} :answer-field/id #uuid "de303eef-640c-5ab4-8dca-8fdfa2e608d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c0e48fc-bca3-50ea-ba63-bf42dda0d6a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-353227" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=13,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=13,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "823136b4-992e-5e49-87a1-3a415027d78a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Constant Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c6c1e82-9215-59d2-82bf-e80a9fc2318b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c6c1e82-9215-59d2-82bf-e80a9fc2318b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337946} :answer-field/id #uuid "8546a5d0-b657-54c6-8ab3-5d3ed29b47b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337945}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1d6b2f9-6480-5dbc-b43c-41f181d02363" :question/math-academy-id "q-353232" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/c1fe71fe51ae93fc2e78b52b1d3f1c270ab05d10c317345bf1440b13c570483f.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=30$ (shown above), evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=30,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{(-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,3\\pi /4} & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2dc5831-1a0d-597b-a2a2-110813a0b40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(\\theta)+\\theta\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2dc5831-1a0d-597b-a2a2-110813a0b40c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(\\theta)+\\theta\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337949} :answer-field/id #uuid "08451976-c5a4-5431-a984-8358a00dcef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d2594a0-4686-55bb-800b-08f075a02c7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-353244" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/89ca909a3e3628e83e6aa4b2a20049ca33bd0436e130017caa9266fbea259022.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{\\theta }{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{(\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[(\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}{\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10f0590c-cfda-5588-a18e-953b08785dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)+\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337952} {:answer/id #uuid "de1f84ca-8187-572d-8cfe-0bf00da7bb3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337953}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10f0590c-cfda-5588-a18e-953b08785dc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin(\\theta)\\cos(\\theta)+\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337952} :answer-field/id #uuid "11dd52f2-c9b0-5f31-a205-0ec502a1fa67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beb8456c-0821-585b-a5f6-e11a32c1809e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353249" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={((\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={((\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)}^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337954 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2037eca-37ca-5750-9c36-b1613c66ff4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ff6be4-f1ab-5df6-b5d5-bad0eb4f965d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337956} {:answer/id #uuid "47984acc-d046-5e2b-bb9b-90699bb2992f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26ff6be4-f1ab-5df6-b5d5-bad0eb4f965d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337956} :answer-field/id #uuid "9525c902-b725-5cd5-bef4-5160c11feccf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2311f571-051b-59c3-846b-c55dfbfb028e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353412" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta },$ evaluate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at $\\theta =0.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,{|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nOur strategy will be to compute ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =0,$ and then compute their ratio.\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r={e}^{\\theta },$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ), \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $\\theta =0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =({e}^{\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $\\theta =0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(0)}{{x}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337958 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X and Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e8959df-c96e-5ad5-a646-6bf11221dc36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337960} {:answer/id #uuid "f479e5e1-c19c-5f25-bb29-23a332200677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337961} {:answer/id #uuid "50e972e0-c7a8-5931-9809-9e025814f3ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337962} {:answer/id #uuid "5918f666-eb21-58b1-8527-d7be44d08f03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337963} {:answer/id #uuid "35711dc4-a7ea-567c-99eb-55df66851d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e8959df-c96e-5ad5-a646-6bf11221dc36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337960} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9c801f8-39c3-55af-acc3-1f1bc481c8d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cbeeb5c2-3495-5b6a-8ea2-6b144a025d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-75272" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/393199da94e9cb9b2a5944ba899e883a12911f66755f2b5407f512626358d944.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta }$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{e}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0a0dd73-d526-529b-9a98-594e8d5825aa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Polar Curve Given the Derivatives With Respect To X an