dent :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f130b6c-2713-5ccf-b7e4-15960d41a37a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75276" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059545/assets/7580d53db6063a859e46addc9635b958e96ad798508dccb0705701d5b36c12b6.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))}{-2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
